# CC.FamilyLinkNewOk counterexample (scratch/orch/linknewpf-STATUS.md).
# F's and G's contexts merge X with T (two readings of A.gimme). Audit's
# world picks the merge representative freely (w_context is a witness);
# take T, for both F and G. F's result key `X.m` is then read at F's inputs
# as T.m. M(F) = {X}, so G's pins (D84 3d) are {X}: G may rebind T.
# G binds T = Bool, then `T.m`, keyed Bool.m. LinkBody's ResultRealizes
# looks the result key up at the body's site, `at_site Cb (T.m)`, which
# re-reads the receiver through G's final table: Bool.m. So Audit accepts
# G, and the closure's result source for position 0 is G's Bool.m.
# main demands Concrete.m (X := Concrete; the merge holds since T =
# Concrete there). The application installs G's Bool.m closure under the
# caller's Concrete.m, and `if this` runs on a Concrete: stuck.
# The trouble is the receiver: a linked input's meaning is the caller's,
# but at_site reads the body's alias for it (bind T = ... writes the alias
# only). Signatures are compared under c_sub only, so they are not hit.
# Oracle: rejects ("functor `G` does not bind `X.m`"); with the other
# context order it also rejects. Native: rejects ("the functor does not
# bind every item its result promises: `m`").
# Variant (scratch/linknewpf/m5-*.moss): G also binds `X.m` (keyed T.m).
# Audit, at representative T, still selects Bool.m. The oracle rejects it
# when its representative is T (its pins include the representative,
# "`bind T` ... changes a type its result mentions (D84 3d)") and accepts
# it when it is X; native accepts both orders and runs them (exit 0).
type Foo;
type A;
type T;
type X;
tag Concrete;
fn .gimme(): Foo;
fn .m();
context Merge = A, X, T, A.gimme[Foo=X], A.gimme[Foo=T];
functor F[X]: Merge -> X.m;
functor G: Merge -> X.m { bind T = Bool; bind T.m = () => { if this { } }; }
fn main() {
  bind A = Concrete;
  bind T = Concrete;
  bind Foo = Concrete;
  bind A.gimme = () => Concrete;
  bind F = G;
  Concrete.m();
}
